+<p><b>Raw cards / Graded cards (November 4).</b> The first row of the tab chooses the cards: <b>Raw cards</b> is the tab as described here, and its <b>Forecasting</b> and <b>Planning</b> buttons are two pages of their own; <b>Graded cards</b> (PSA and CGC) has its own Forecasting and Planning. Each of the four pages has its own address, and the tab reopens on the one you used last.</p>
+<p><b>Graded cards &gt; Forecasting (November 4).</b> For buying slabs at a vendor show and selling them on eBay: every graded row of the PriceCharting tab in your price band is listed and assumed for sale. The odds are this tab's method run on PriceCharting's monthly graded lines (PSA 10, Grade 9.5 / 9 / 8 / 7, since 2021): a slab's rank is how far its grade's price sits below its own 12-month mean (ten groups), its Chance how often slabs of that group made money over the hold (1 to 6 months) in this kind of market, 2022 on. <b>Buy price</b> = standard price x (1 - % under) - the eBay Shipping Fee, the most to pay the vendor; at the default 14.1% under (eBay's fee) a slab whose price does not move nets $0, so the profit is the move and anything bought deeper under. Standard price, Buy price, Sold, Net and ROI show Card Ladder over PriceCharting; the Chance is PriceCharting's. The filter buttons are the PriceCharting tab's with this page's own settings. Known flaws, for the graded model to come: PriceCharting's Grade 7 - 9.5 lines blend every grader, and grades without a line of their own (CGC 10, half grades, 1 - 6) have no score. Graded Planning is still to come; + keeps the slabs for it.</p>
 
